"""
PCOS prediction from survey data: refined analysis pipeline
============================================================

Exploratory machine learning analysis of a short online questionnaire on
polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).

Dataset: n = 99 respondents, 14 raw columns.
Target:  "Have you been medically diagnosed with PCOS?" -> 40 Yes / 59 No.

Usage
-----
    python pcos_pipeline.py --data data/pcos_survey.xlsx --out results

Both paths can also be set with the environment variables PCOS_DATA and
PCOS_RESULTS. The script also runs cell by cell in Jupyter.

Read this before interpreting any output
----------------------------------------
With 99 respondents and 40 positive cases, this dataset supports an
exploratory, descriptive analysis. It does not support a claim that a tuned
gradient boosting model is a validated PCOS screening tool. The
events-per-predictor ratio is approximately 4 at best, well below the ~10-20
conventionally required for stable model estimation. Every performance number
is reported with an uncertainty interval, and model families are compared
against each other, against logistic regression, and against a
majority-class baseline.

Corrections relative to the original script
-------------------------------------------
1. Criterion leakage. Menstrual cycle regularity, irregular periods, acne,
   and facial/body hair growth are components of the Rotterdam diagnostic
   criteria. Two pre-specified feature sets are therefore run and reported:
   SET_A (lifestyle and demographic only) and SET_B (SET_A plus
   self-reported symptoms).
2. Optimistic tuning bias. Hyperparameters are selected with nested
   cross-validation, so tuning happens inside the training portion only.
3. Height and weight parsing. Heights such as "5'4", "5 feet 3 inches",
   5.4 (five feet four inches), "158 cm", "5.1 inches", "5,2\"",
   "5/2 or 5/3", and "4.4 ft", and weights such as "58 kg" and "40kg", are
   parsed explicitly instead of becoming missing and being median-imputed.
4. Symptom information. The multi-select symptom field is expanded into
   binary indicators rather than collapsed to a count.

The diagnostic test column and the test results column are excluded as
outcome leakage. The test column is missing for 31 respondents, and that
missingness is itself informative, so imputing it would import the outcome
into the model.

Data availability
-----------------
The survey workbook contains respondents' health information and is not
distributed with this repository. Outputs in the results directory include
row-level data (out-of-fold probabilities and the analysis dataset) and must
not be committed.
"""

